Struct:
 - A struct is a user-defined data type that groups related fields, potentially of different types, into a single unit.
 - type Employee struct {
     	Name string
     	Age  int
   }
 - zero value initialization - var e3 Employee

Struct embedding:
 - Struct embedding allows you to include a type inside another struct without explicitly specifying a field name
 - package main
   import "fmt"
   
   type Address struct {
       City    string
       Country string
   }
   
   type User struct {
       Name string
       Address // Embedded struct
   }
   
   func main() {
       u := User{
           Name: "Rahul",
           Address: Address{
               City:    "Hyderabad",
               Country: "India",
           },
       }
   
       fmt.Println(u.Name)
       fmt.Println(u.City)
       fmt.Println(u.Address.City)
   }

Embedding methods:
 - An embedded type's methods can also be promoted to the outer type.
 - type Logger struct{}
    func (Logger) Log(msg string) {
        fmt.Println("LOG:", msg)
    }
    
    type Service struct {
        Logger
    }
    
    func main() {
        s := Service{}
        s.Log("request received")
    }

Method Sets:
 - A method set is the set of methods associated with a type for the purpose of method selection and interface implementation.
 - type User struct{}
   
   func (User) Name() string {
       return "Rahul"
   }
   
   func (*User) Update() {
       fmt.Println("updated")
   }
 - Pointer receiver receives both value and pointer while value receiver rejects pointer

- Embedding promotes fields and methods; it does not create a subtype relationship.
- The embedded field can still be accessed explicitly, such as s.Logger.
- If the outer type defines a method with the same name as a promoted method, the outer method takes precedence for that selector.
- If multiple embedded fields create an ambiguous selector, you must qualify the field explicitly.
- Struct A and Struct B, A has B and both A and B implements C, then a.C is seperate from b.C
- Struct A and Struct B, A has named B and both B implements C, then a.b.C and b.C works
- If A implements method C() and A contains B, but B does not implement C(), then:
  - a.C() works.
  - b.C() gives a compile-time error.

Pointer receivers vs Value receivers
 - In Go, methods can have either value receivers or pointer receivers.

 - A value receiver receives a copy of the struct, so modifications to the receiver's fields do not affect the original struct.
   A pointer receiver receives a copy of a pointer to the original struct, 
   allowing the method to modify the original state without copying the entire struct.

 - I prefer pointer receivers when a method needs to mutate state, when copying a large struct is undesirable,
   or when the struct contains synchronization primitives such as a mutex. Value receivers are appropriate for small, 
   value-like types when copy semantics are desirable.

 - An important distinction is method sets. A type `T` includes methods declared with value receivers,
   whereas `*T` includes methods declared with both value and pointer receivers. 
   This distinction affects interface implementation, even though Go can automatically take the address of an addressable value when calling a pointer-receiver method.

 - Finally, pointer receivers do not inherently mean heap allocation. Escape analysis determines whether a value escapes to the heap.
